# SRG-APP-000705-AU-000110  -  CAT II
# The Central Log Server must disable accounts when the accounts are no
# longer associated to a user.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000705-AU-000110
group:           V-263557
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       manual
confidence:      derived
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-003628
control_rev5:    AC-2

contribution:    Splunk cannot itself determine that an account no longer belongs to a
                 user; it keeps the path open on which an external identity authority's
                 disable or delete arrives, and surfaces the trace a departed owner leaves
                 behind in scheduled searches.

external:        The enterprise directory service or identity provider determines when an
                 account is no longer associated to a user and must push the resulting
                 disable or delete; Splunk honors it at the next authentication.
external:        Where accounts are provisioned through SCIM, the provisioning authority
                 issues the deprovisioning call.
external:        The organization's offboarding procedure, and the review that finds
                 accounts with no owner, are procedural and outside the application.
external:        An assessor may use Splunk's orphaned scheduled search notification as
                 evidence that departed owners are detected, but it notifies rather than
                 disables.

manual_reason:   No setting in the 10.4.2 specification set determines that an account is
                 no longer associated to a user, or disables it on that basis, so
                 configuration cannot decide this requirement.

citation:        limits.conf [system_checks] orphan_searches "Enables/disables automatic UI
                 message notifications to admins for scheduled saved searches with invalid
                 owners" and defaults to enabled (limits.conf.spec:4486-4498). It raises a
                 message about a search; it disables no account, so equals:enabled yields a
                 confident status for a requirement it does not touch, and because enabled
                 is already the default the condition can only fail an instance that has
                 turned the notification off - a finding over a UI message, not over
                 account disabling. The server.conf [teleport_supervisor] disabled and
                 web-features.conf [feature:identity_sidecar_scim] enabled conditions are
                 the SCIM token process and the SAML dialog toggle described under
                 SRG-APP-000700-WSR-000100: both already sit in the state the criterion
                 demands by default (server.conf.spec:6584-6591,
                 web-features.conf.spec:400-410), neither disables an account, and neither
                 appears in any check or fix text, since this SRG carries no product STIG
                 rule. The inventory's scimSupportedDomains and scsSyncUserDeletes keys
                 exist in no .conf.spec in the 10.4.2 set. Nothing in the product expresses
                 disassociation, so this is case 4: manual, with the lifecycle authority
                 named in the external dependencies rather than asserted through a proxy
                 setting.
finding:         No application-layer finding is available: Splunk has no setting that
                 detects an account with no associated user or disables it, so the assessor
                 records the identity authority that performs the disable; raise a finding
                 only where no authority outside the application does so.
